  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 104 PM CDT Tue Jul 14 2026\n\nStrong ridging centered across the Upper Midwest will gradually \nweaken Wednesday into Thursday with a transition to more pronounced \nnorthwesterly flow this weekend into next week. Temperatures will \nslowly fall back over the coming days, although will remain \nabove normal into the weekend. \n\nA weak front will slide south on Wednesday with drier air across \nnorthern Wisconsin providing some relief from the humidity. With \nparts of central into east-central Wisconsin likely to remain quite \nhumid with highs in the low to mid 90s, and the cumulative impacts \nof multiple days of hot weather, will extend the heat advisory \nthrough Wednesday evening for some counties south of Highway 29.\nHowever, temperatures may be impacted slightly by wildfire\nsmoke, depending on the degree of smoke.\n\nThunderstorm chances:\n\nLarge scale ascent for storms will be weak through Wednesday. \nHowever, there is a low chance (<20%) for a storm to sneak into \nnorthern Wisconsin this evening on the fringe of the stronger flow \naloft. Similarly, a very small chance for a storm exists \nWednesday afternoon across central Wisconsin in the vicinity of \nthe weak boundary and higher instability. Given the limited \nforcing and shear, any storms will be disorganized and \ntransient. \n\nThere is a greater signal for showers and storms at times later\nThursday through the weekend as the ridge breaks down while \nseasonably high moisture values remain in place. There are \ngenerally low to medium (20-60%) chances for showers and storms \nintermittently into next week within the warm, moderately \nunstable environment, but predictability of waves/greater \nforcing within the northwest flow is low. Machine learning \nguidance shows at least low (<15%) chances for severe weather \nfor parts of the area Friday through Monday, with deep layer \nflow sufficient for organized storms. However, these details \nwill be better resolved in coming days. \n\nSmoke: \n\nAs boundary layer flow becomes northerly tonight into Wednesday, \nsmoke from wildfires across northeast Minnesota into southern \nOntario will slide south across the area. At the very least, \nskies will take on a more hazy appearance, and depending on \nsmoke concentrations and the degree of near surface smoke, may \nimpact air quality and visibility. The Wisconsin Department of \nNatural Resources has issued an Air Quality Advisory through \nnoon Thursday due to impacts from the smoke.",
